# Core Principles

## Philosophy
- SOLID, KISS, DRY — prefer simplicity and readability over premature optimization
- Functional and declarative patterns; encapsulate state in modules, not globals
- Minimal, focused diffs: change only what the task requires
- Deliver complete implementations without placeholders in new code

## Precedence
When rules conflict: (1) explicit user instruction, (2) more specific glob rule, (3) this file.

## Naming
- Directories and files: kebab-case (`features/auth-wizard/`, `login-form.js`)
- Functions: camelCase; event handlers prefixed with `handle` (`handleSubmit`)
- Booleans: prefixed with `is`, `has`, `can` (`isLoading`, `hasError`)
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for true constants (`const API_BASE_URL = ...`)
- DOM hooks: `data-*` attributes for JS selectors (`data-login-form`, `data-action`)

## Code Style
- Early returns and guard clauses; happy path last
- ES modules (`import`/`export`); one responsibility per file
- Prefer `const`; use `let` only when reassignment is needed
- Avoid `var`
- Comment only non-obvious business logic; let names document intent
- Module entrypoints: `export function initX(...)` or `export function createX(...)`; standalone callbacks may use `const`

## Testing
- New modules in `features/` ship with a co-located `*.test.js`; see `testing-standards.mdc`

## Workflow
- Complex tasks: brief plan, then implement
- Clear tasks: implement directly
- Do not ask for confirmation unless the user requested a plan or choice

## Legacy Code
- No TODOs in new code you deliver
- Out-of-scope legacy bugs: document with TODO only when not part of current task
